cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "Local Degree Heterogeneity Predicts Functional Variability Gradients in the Human Connectome"
authors:
  - family-names: "Magnusson"
    given-names: "Morten"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0002-4860-5095"
    affiliation: "Symbiose Research, Sandnes, Norway"
date-released: "2026-04-05"
doi: "10.6084/m9.figshare.31940370"
url: "https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.31940370"
repository-code: "https://github.com/supertedai/EFC"
license: "CC-BY-4.0"
type: "article"
keywords:
  - "structural connectome"
  - "functional variability"
  - "hub-periphery gradient"
  - "degree heterogeneity"
  - "Fiedler eigenvalue"
  - "biomarker"
abstract: >-
  Hub regions in the human brain exhibit higher functional variability
  than peripheral regions. We quantify this gradient using the centrifugal
  entropy score kappa and show across two independent datasets that it is
  driven by local degree heterogeneity (r = -0.97, p < 0.001), not global
  algebraic connectivity (Fiedler eigenvalue; r = 0.16, p = 0.70).
